class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/en/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/en/stories/">Takua Pa 
Stories</a><span>/</span><span>Old houses and architecture</span></div><span class="eyebrow">Takua Pa stories · Old 
houses and architecture</span><h1>Buildings built with tin money and dredger steel · Architecture that remembers the 
town</h1><p class="lead">Read Takua Pa through the shophouses on Culture Street, Boon Sung Iron Bridge, the governor's 
residence, and Tao Ming School · The transition embedded in brick, mortar, and steel structures</p><p 
class="story-meta">Updated on 2026-09-07 · 6-minute read</p></header><figure class="hero-photo-v2"><img 
src="/assets/photos/tao-ming-school.jpg" alt="อาคารโรงเรียนเต้าหมิง ถนนสายวัฒนธรรมเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" 
loading="eager"><figcaption><span>Tao Ming School seen from the front, on the old town culture street</span><span 
class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><div class="story-layout-v2"><div 
class="story-main-v2"><div class="story-lede-v2"><p>If you walk through the Thanon Udom Thara and Thanon Si Takua Pa 
areas today, the first sight is rows of Sino-Portuguese shophouses. The front walkway has a roof cover known as 
&#x27;Ngo Ka Ki&#x27; which provides shade and rain protection for pedestrians. It feels like stepping back in time to 
a well-preserved past.</p><p>But these buildings are not just beautiful backdrops for photography. They are a massive 
archive recording the changes of Takua Pa in the form of brick, mortar, and steel structures. Each piece of 
architecture tells the story of a town that grew and prospered through tin mining, water trade, and the blending of 
people from diverse cultures.</p><p>To deeply understand Takua Pa's architecture, we must look beyond its external 
beauty. We need to distinguish between what is &#x27;newly built&#x27; and what is &#x27;repaired&#x27;, and 
understand the layers of history hidden within the governor's residence walls, the miners' iron bridge, and the school 
built from merchants' donations.</p></div><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-0">The governor's 
residence and the wall that tells of conflict</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img 
src="/assets/culture-street.webp" alt="ถนนสายวัฒนธรรมเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Takua Pa Old 
Town Cultural Street</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>Back in the era when Takua 
Pa was growing from the tin mining economy and coastal trade, wealth came with conflicts of interest. The wall of the 
Takua Pa governor's residence was built to act like a camp wall, protecting against danger and serving as a refuge for 
townspeople. Scholars suggest, though not settled, that this wall was built during the time when Phraya Senanuchit 
(Nuch Na Nakhon) was the governor between 1840 (2383 BE) and 1881.</p><p>The wall was constructed with plaster mixed 
with gravel and sand, layered with a base wider than the wall itself for stability. The part that remains today is 
about 5 meters high. The inner area was once divided into a front palace, a main hall, and a rear palace, reflecting 
the systematic space allocation of the town's rulers in that era.</p><p>Important evidence confirming the prosperity 
of trade in that era comes from the archaeological excavation by the Fine Arts Department in 1999, which found Qing 
dynasty Chinese ceramics, European ceramics, glassware, and building decorative parts. These items confirm that this 
governor's residence was once connected to a global trade network.</p><p>When the city center moved to Ko Kho Khao and 
Ban Yan Yao, the former governor's residence lost its administrative role. However, today this area has become an open 
archaeological site that the community uses for cultural events, such as the &#x27;From Takola to Takua Pa&#x27; 
festival, bringing the old walls back to life as the city's stage.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span 
class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">The year the wall construction began</span></div></section><blockquote 
class="pull-quote-v2"><p>Architecture is not just a beautiful backdrop. It is an archive recording the changes of 
Takua Pa in the form of brick, mortar, and steel structures.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 
id="section-1">The district chief's house in the transition era</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img 
src="/assets/governor-wall.webp" alt="กำแพงจวนเจ้าเมืองตะกั่วป่า" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Governor’s 
Residence Wall</span><span class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The transition from the governor 
system to the district system was recorded in the architecture of &#x27;Ban Khun In&#x27;. According to local 
evidence, this house was built around 1917 (B.E. 2460) by Chinese craftsmen to be the residence of Lieutenant Khun 
Intharakhiri (Choi na Nakhon), a descendant of the governor lineage who later became the Takua Pa district 
chief.</p><p>The building is a two-story brick and masonry structure in an applied Sino-Portuguese style, reflecting 
the architectural influence from port cities like Penang and Singapore that spread through maritime trade. This house 
therefore not only displays the status of its owner but also serves as evidence showing Takua Pa's connection to the 
outside world.</p><p>In reading history from architecture, we must be careful to distinguish between the year of 
construction and the year of repair. A report from the Office of Natural Resources and Environmental Policy and 
Planning indicates that the Na Nakhon family repaired this house in 1981, trying to preserve the original style but 
changing the roofing material to corrugated tiles.</p><p>Knowing this repair information helps us see Ban Khun In not 
just as a static past, but as a living structure that has been maintained and improved by its residents over many 
decades.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span class="story-flag-v2 flag-unverified">The year Ban Khun In was built 
(1917)</span></div></section><blockquote class="pull-quote-v2"><p>From an industrial structure, the Boon Sung Iron 
Bridge has changed its role to a community thoroughfare and has become a major landmark attracting people to visit the 
traces of the mining town.</p></blockquote><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-2">Educational funds from 
mining and merchant networks</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/iron-bridge.webp" 
alt="สะพานเหล็กบุญสูง" loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Boon Soong Iron Bridge</span><span 
class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>The wealth from tin mining was not only spent on building 
private residences, but also led to the creation of community institutions. The &#x27;Tao Ming School&#x27; building, 
constructed in 1922 (B.E. 2465), is a clear example of community collaboration.</p><p>Funds for constructing this 
school came from fundraising among wealthy miners and Chinese merchants from the Old Town market, Yan Yao, Ranong, and 
Phuket. This shows the strong geographical and economic network of the Chinese community in this region.</p><p>The 
school building was built by Chinese craftsmen in an applied Sino-Portuguese style and operated as a primary school 
for a long time. It served as a center for passing on knowledge, language, and culture to generations of Thai-Chinese 
children in Takua Pa.</p><p>Even though the school has ceased its teaching operations today, the building is still 
maintained by the Takua Pa Tao Ming School Foundation, standing as a memorial to the community's commitment to 
education during the town's golden era.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 id="section-3">From a tin 
dredger to a workers' bridge</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img src="/assets/khun-in.webp" alt="บ้านขุนอินทร์" 
loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Khun In House</span><span 
class="credit">Illustration</span></figcaption></figure><p>While residences and schools reflect the wealth of 
merchants, the &#x27;Saphan Lek Bun Sung&#x27; (Bun Sung Iron Bridge) tells the story of the industrial era and the 
working class. This bridge was built in 1968 (B.E. 2511) and is a direct product of the city's tin mining 
industry.</p><p>Before the bridge existed, miners and villagers had to rely on rowboats to cross the Takua Pa Canal, 
which is over 40 meters wide, making it extremely difficult during the flood season. Although a wooden bridge for 
pedestrians and bicycles had been built, as the industry expanded, the demand for a stronger structure 
increased.</p><p>The Juti Boon Sung Dredging Company initiated the construction of this bridge by assembling the steel 
frames from a decommissioned dredger to form the Khok Khanun iron bridge, which is over 200 meters long. This bridge 
became an important shortcut connecting the Old Town market area to the mining area, facilitating labor and 
transportation.</p><p>Although the sound of mining machinery has long gone silent today, the iron bridge remains. It 
has changed its role from an industrial structure to a community thoroughfare and has become a major landmark that 
attracts people to visit the traces of the Takua Pa mining town.</p></section><section class="story-section-v2"><h2 
id="section-4">Culture Street and shophouses brought back to life</h2><figure class="section-photo-v2"><img 
src="/assets/photos/shophouse-78.webp" alt="ตึกแถวเลขที่ 78 บนถนนสายวัฒนธรรมเมืองเก่าตะกั่วป่า" 
loading="lazy"><figcaption><span>Shophouse number 78 on the old town culture street</span><span 
class="credit">Kuapapoh project archive</span></figcaption></figure><p>The shophouses along both sides of Udom Tara 
and Sri Takua Pa streets are the town's most prominent architectural heritage. These buildings were built to align 
with the commercial lifestyle, featuring ground-floor spaces for business and connected Ngo Ka Ki walkways. These 
buildings were influenced by Straits colonial architecture, reflecting the trade networks with Penang and 
Singapore.</p><p>After the tin mining industry declined and the town's commercial center moved away, this Old Town 
market area faced quietness. Many buildings were left to decay over time, as if the golden era of this street had 
passed.</p><p>However, the surviving architecture has become an important asset in revitalizing this area. Around 2007 
(B.E. 2550), the &#x27;Takua Pa Old Town Reminiscing&#x27; project was initiated, piloting the opening of the street 
space as a cultural walking street before developing into the current Takua Pa Old Town Cultural Street.</p><p>Every 
Sunday, this street is closed to traffic and opens as a bustling market. The old Sino-Portuguese buildings return to 
their role as a backdrop for community activities, including the selling of local food, crafts, and performances, 
proving that past architecture can find new life and roles in the present day.</p><div class="story-flags-v2"><span 
class="story-flag-v2 flag-disputed">The year the walking street project started</span></div></section><section 
class="story-places-v2"><h2>Where to visit in person</h2><div class="mini-cards-v2"><div 
